CORRECTION #13  (2026-08-25)  FILL QUALITY COMES FROM FILL-TIME RECORDS, NOT QUOTE SNAPSHOTS
  What was wrong: the 8/24 review judged fill quality off a quote-table snapshot taken ~2s
    BEFORE the trade (msgoptionimpliedquote nbbo=1.50/2.20 at 14:55:01.222) and concluded
    "1220 lots filled instantly off a surface the book was not showing." Zach: that NBBO "is
    completely wrong... SPXW zero-day option prices move very volatile, especially around the
    last ten minutes of the day. It's not a surprise that the NBBO would flip from these
    higher values to the lower values when we executed." SpiderRock's own fill records show
    fillBid 0.80-0.85 at execution; every print was inside its bid/ask.
  THE RULE, in order:
    1. Data source, v7: srtrade_agm_<YYYY_MM_DD>.msgsrparentexecution (198.102.4.55:3307).
       Data source, v8: srtrade_<YYYY_MM_DD>.msgsrparentexecution (192.81.231.66:3700).
       NOTE THE NAMING: v8 DROPS the _agm_ infix -- srtrade_2026_08_25, not
       srtrade_agm_2026_08_25, which does not exist on v8 at all. Same table name either side.
       Confirmed with SpiderRock (Will) 2026-08-26. v8 accounts (e.g. EOD61_C42, the
       355 SPREAD_BUYS book) do NOT appear in the v7 table, so both hosts must be queried to
       see the whole day's fills.
       RETRY RULE: the v8 endpoint is load-balanced and some backends lack the grant, so a
       query can come back "ERROR 1142 SELECT command denied" on one connection and succeed on
       the next. Measured 2026-08-26: 8 of 12 fresh connections succeeded. On an error or a
       denial, WAIT 5 SECONDS AND RETRY on a new connection -- do not treat the first failure
       as the table being unavailable. A backend with the grant lists 47 databases; one
       without lists 5, which is a quick way to tell them apart.
       Only report the table as inaccessible (and loudly, per Zach's standing ask) if it still
       fails after several spaced retries.
       Query it EVERY review cycle for every option algo that filled. fillBid / fillAsk are
       the quote AT the fill -- the only valid evidence for fill quality. A pre-trade quote
       snapshot from the script log is context for why a limit was set, never evidence of
       fill quality.
    2. What to flag: a fill is POTENTIALLY TOO AGGRESSIVE when BOTH hold --
       (a) fillPrice sits at/near the disadvantageous side (near fillBid on a sell, near
           fillAsk on a buy), AND
       (b) the fillBid/fillAsk spread is extremely wide.
       Zach's calibration: sold at 0.82 on fillBid 0.80 / fillAsk 0.95 = NOT a bad fill.
       Sold at 0.82 on fillBid 0.80 / fillAsk 1.20 = flag it.
    3. When in doubt, ALERT -- err toward flagging a potentially too-aggressive fill.
    4. Citation: name the exact table, e.g. srtrade_agm_2026_08_24.msgsrparentexecution.
    5. ACCESS FAILURE IS ITSELF A FINDING (Zach, 2026-08-25). If this table cannot be
       reached on any review -- connect error, database or table missing for the trade
       date, or empty rows on a day the logs show option fills (e.g. trading has moved
