CORRECTION #15  (2026-08-25)  DECOMPOSE THE PROD-VS-BACKTEST GAP: CLOSER PRECEDENCE AND LOSS CAP
  Zach: in future reviews, break out how much of the PnL difference between backtest and prod
    comes from (a) the CLOSER precedence resize and (b) the script loss-cap pro-rata --
    i.e. state the dollar figures for each, like the -$52,360 and -$14,280 on 8/24.
  How to compute (the 8/24 worked example):
    (a) CLOSER PRECEDENCE: prod moves size from the parent to the CLOSER (Resize line in the
        script log, e.g. "GROWS 1068 -> 320, offset by CLOSER size 748"); the backtest keeps
        the parent at full size AND counts the CLOSER, double-counting the offset lots.
        Dollar figure = offset lots x backtest per-lot PnL. 8/24: 748 x $70 = -$52,360.
    (b) LOSS-CAP PRO-RATA: the [LossCap] PRO-RATA line gives pre -> post sizes per trade
        (e.g. 356/320/748 -> 305/274/641, ~0.857x); the backtest does not model it.
        Dollar figure = lots removed x backtest per-lot PnL. 8/24: 204 x $70 = -$14,280.
    (c) The residual after (a) and (b) is fill-price difference -- state it only if it is
        large enough to matter under the correction #7 bar.
    Per-lot PnL comes from the backtest sheet (total_pnl / size); when put and call per-lot
    differ, decompose per side. Sources: the script log's Resize and [LossCap] lines (both
    already in the digest's per-script section) plus the backtest workbook.
  This slots into the correction #14 shape: aggregate prod vs aggregate backtest first, then
    the (a)/(b) dollar breakouts as the explanation of the gap, then any per-algo flag.
  RIGHT (8/24 form):
